{"causes":["The Cold War created an environment where Southeast Asian nations sought greater autonomy and cooperation. Rising nationalism among member states pushed for stronger regional integration.","A series of military coups and communist insurgencies in the region highlighted the need for stability and security measures."],"effects":["ASEAN's creation led to a significant increase in regional trade, with the association fostering economic cooperation and growth among its member states. The association has also promoted cultural exchange, tourism, and people-to-people diplomacy.","The establishment of ASEAN led to the development of several regional institutions, including the Association of Southeast Asian Nations Secretariat (ASEANSec), which coordinates the work of regional bodies such as the ASEAN Economic Community (AEC) and the ASEAN Political-Security Community (APSC).","ASEAN has played a crucial role in promoting democracy and human rights in Southeast Asia, with its member states working together to strengthen their institutions and promote good governance."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Southeast-Asian-Cold-War","sourceName":"Cold War","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"The Cold War created an environment where Southeast Asian nations sought greater autonomy and cooperation."},{"sourceSlug":"Nationalism-Southeast-Asia","sourceName":"Nationalism in Southeast Asia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"Rising nationalism among member states pushed for stronger regional integration."},{"sourceSlug":"Korea-War-Southeast-Asia","sourceName":"Korean War and Southeast Asia","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"The Korean War (1950-1953) highlighted the importance of regional cooperation in Southeast Asia."},{"sourceSlug":"United-Nations-Southeast-Asia","sourceName":"United Nations in Southeast Asia","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"The Association participates in the United Nations system to promote its interests and contribute to global governance."},{"sourceSlug":"Non-Aligned-Movement-Southeast-Asia","sourceName":"Non-Aligned Movement in Southeast Asia","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"ASEAN collaborates with the Non-Aligned Movement to promote regional cooperation and counterbalance the influence of major powers."},{"sourceSlug":"US-ASEAN-Economic-Ties","sourceName":"United States in ASEAN Economic Relations","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"The United States has significant economic ties with ASEAN, with bilateral trade and investment relationships providing a foundation for regional cooperation."},{"sourceSlug":"Singapore-Economic-Miracle","sourceName":"Singapore Economic Miracle","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"ASEAN's founding member Singapore served as a model for economic development and integration."},{"sourceSlug":"Indonesian-Independence","sourceName":"Indonesian Independence","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations","targetName":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations","context":"The Indonesian independence movement (1945-1966) laid the groundwork for ASEAN's creation."}],"places":[{"name":"Bangkok, Thailand","role":"Headquarters"},{"name":"Singapore","role":"Founding Member"},{"name":"Jakarta, Indonesia","role":"Birthplace"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Association of Southeast Asian Nations","type":"Reference article","slug":"association-of-southeast-asian-nations"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7768","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASEAN"],"tags":["intergovernmental organization","Political Systems & Governance","political-theory"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Association of Southeast Asian Nations is remembered as a landmark idea of the Contemporary era, referenced in 164 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.","summary":"The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) is a regional intergovernmental organisation founded on 8 August 1967 in Bangkok, Thailand — established by the Bangkok Declaration, signed by the five founding members: Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, and Thailand. As of 2024, ASEAN has ten member states (adding Brunei, Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar, and Cambodia over subsequent decades), with a combined population of approximately 680 million and a collective GDP of approximately $3.6 trillion — making it the third-largest economy in Asia and the fifth-largest in the world. ASEAN operates under the principle of non-interference in members' internal affairs and consensus-based decision-making — principles that reflect both Southeast Asian diplomatic traditions and the Cold War context of its founding.

ASEAN was founded in the context of the Cold War — specifically in the aftermath of the Indonesian communist coup attempt of 1965 (the September 30th Movement), the ongoing Vietnam War, and the general concern among non-communist Southeast Asian governments about regional stability and communist expansion. The founding members were all non-communist, and the organisation was partly designed to provide a framework for cooperation among the non-communist states of the region. However, ASEAN's explicit focus on economic cooperation and non-interference in political affairs distinguished it from more overtly political Cold War alliances like SEATO (Southeast Asia Treaty Organisation), which it effectively outlasted.

Over its five decades, ASEAN has evolved from a modest Cold War regional arrangement into one of the world's most significant regional organisations — institutionalising the 'ASEAN Way' of dialogue and consensus, establishing the ASEAN Free Trade Area (AFTA, 1992), developing the ASEAN Community framework (political-security, economic, and socio-cultural pillars), and launching the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP, 2022) — the world's largest free trade agreement by population and GDP covered. ASEAN has also become the nucleus of the broader 'ASEAN+3' framework engaging China, Japan, and South Korea, and the East Asia Summit engaging the major Indo-Pacific powers.","subjects":["Southeast Asia","Regional Organisation","Contemporary Era","Geopolitics","East Asia","Cold War","Economic Integration","International Relations"],"frameworks":["WORLD_SYSTEMS","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"ASEAN's creation marked a significant turning point in regional history, fostering cooperation and integration among Southeast Asian nations. While facing challenges such as rising nationalism and economic inequality, ASEAN remains a vital regional player.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
